  "Question": "What is the parity of \\(f(x)=x^2+x\\)?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Neither odd nor even",
    "B": "Odd",
    "C": "Even",
    "D": "Both odd and even"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "\\(f(-x)=x^2-x\\), which equals neither \\(f(x)\\) nor \\(-f(x)\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Disprove both identities with one pair\n\nAn even function must satisfy \\(f(-x)=f(x)\\) for every real \\(x\\), while an odd function must satisfy \\(f(-x)=-f(x)\\). A single failed pair can rule out both: \\(f(1)=2\\) and \\(f(-1)=0\\). Since \\(0\\ne2\\) and \\(0\\ne-2\\), the function is neither odd nor even, choice A.",
